\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Technology\",\"Data Analysis\",\"Research Paper\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"4ff6ba88616fcf7a71d1dca6c9adc14c\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\2403.19401.pdf\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\b6e6995c4481f9cd5d6cfb8c79124693.pdf\",\"priority\":1,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":14092,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.609\",\"description\":\"A One Day International (ODI) is a format of limited overs cricket played between teams with international status, typically consisting of 50 overs per side. Originating in the late twentieth century, the first ODI was played in 1971 between Australia and England after rain affected a Test match. Initially, ODIs featured white kits and a red ball. The late 1970s saw significant innovations through Kerry Packer's World Series Cricket, introducing colored uniforms, night games under floodlights using a white ball, and enhanced television broadcasts with multiple camera angles and audio effects. These changes increased professionalism in the sport, with international players receiving payments and TV rights shifting to Channel 9 in Australia. Over time, colored kits and the white ball became the norm, with the traditional white flannels and red ball phased out by 2001. ODIs are considered the pinnacle of List A cricket, with the format used in major tournaments like the Cricket World Cup.\",\"docName\":\"3nql1cs15r8aviz39pth2bpsr4zvy1\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:57:34.270\",\"fileName\":\"3nql1cs15r8aviz39pth2bpsr4zvy1.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987776371027480576,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Sport\",\"Informational\",\"History\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"da0c0f7162b267cca0de596df4c40c56\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3nql1cs15r8aviz39pth2bpsr4zvy1.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\db8ff9222bdd0e56ba076310ced4c747.pdf\",\"priority\":2,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":402,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.639\",\"description\":\"Psychological identity encompasses self-image, self-esteem, and individuality, representing how individuals perceive themselves in the present, past, and future. According to Weinreich, identity is the totality of self-construal, reflecting continuity across time and aspirations. Ethnic identity, for example, is defined as the part of self-construal connecting ancestry and future goals in terms of ethnicity. The concept of identity is central to fields like psychology, sociology, and anthropology, requiring frameworks that address both unique personal attributes and shifting group affiliations. Weinreich’s Identity Structure Analysis (ISA) provides a structural approach to mapping identity, focusing on stable relationships between the self and others within socio-cultural contexts. ISA utilizes constructs from individual experience, group dynamics, and cultural norms, offering a methodology for analyzing how individuals use these elements to evaluate themselves, others, and institutions over time and in varying circumstances.\",\"docName\":\"3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:57:38.411\",\"fileName\":\"3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987776371153309696,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Informational\",\"Research Paper\",\"Psychology\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"a80f0ad4af9fd250a462680c85e6fbf0\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\4bb6e885c6b375f7991ed237ae550f5a.pdf\",\"priority\":3,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":414,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.666\",\"description\":\"Archie receives orders to join the earl and a large group of soldiers in pursuit of Bruce after a traitor reveals Bruce’s recent whereabouts. Despite his reluctance, Archie prepares to lead the party using Hector, the hound, whose tracking skills are crucial for the search. Archie contemplates killing the hound and fleeing if they get close to Bruce, though escape seems unlikely given the number of mounted men. Guided by a local peasant, the group reaches a cottage five miles away where Hector becomes highly excited, thoroughly investigating the area before decisively taking up Bruce’s trail. Archie struggles to control the eager hound, who pulls hard on the chain. He cautions Pembroke, the earl, to keep the knights and soldiers at a distance, noting that their noise further agitates Hector and makes the dog harder to manage. The narrative details the tension and urgency of the pursuit, highlighting Archie’s internal conflict and the hound’s pivotal role.\",\"docName\":\"3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:57:41.133\",\"fileName\":\"3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987776371266555904,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"History\",\"Report\",\"Text Document\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"bb28043dd606535bdcaf1ee30346b0f9\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\e6e2343232ca421ef2da852a5f84548c.pdf\",\"priority\":4,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":326,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.695\",\"description\":\"\",\"docName\":\"\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:57:37.443\",\"fileName\":\"AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design.pptx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987776371388190720,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:46:00\",\"md5\":\"b0f396e7936e0f6192cd880927e8e74b\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design.pptx\",\"priority\":5,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"RUNNING\",\"tokenNum\":0,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.726\",\"description\":\"\",\"docName\":\"\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.726\",\"errorDescription\":\"\",\"fileName\":\"A_brief_introduction_of_LP_liutao21_20220615.pptx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987776371518214144,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:46:00\",\"md5\":\"0dbb2532524843af54fb3388b7bf3381\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\A_brief_introduction_of_LP_liutao21_20220615.pptx\",\"priority\":6,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"ADDED\",\"tokenNum\":0,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.800\",\"description\":\"\",\"docName\":\"\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11