2025-11-10 14:58:03,412 - [Quantum_e2e_20251110.py:310] - [get_register_document] - INFO - 获取到的result_text: {"jobId":44,"status":"complete","data":{"text":"[{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.563\",\"description\":\"This paper studies the computational hardness of learning Boolean functions from label proportions (LLP), a machine learning setting where only aggregated label information within small subsets (bags) is available. LLP generalizes the PAC learning framework and is motivated by applications like privacy-preserving learning and domains with high labeling costs (e.g., medical imaging). The authors prove three main results: (1) It is NP-hard to learn an ℓ-clause CNF that satisfies any constant-fraction of bags when the underlying function is an OR, establishing hardness even for bags of size ≤2 and separating CNFs from halfspaces. (2) It is NP-hard to learn an ℓ-DNF that satisfies more than (1/2 + δ)-fraction of bags for any constant δ>0 and ℓ, ruling out constant-factor DNF approximations. (3) For parities, with bags of size ≤q, it is NP-hard to satisfy more than (q/2^{q-1} + δ)-fraction of the bags, with the hardness increasing exponentially in bag size. However, a randomized polynomial-time algorithm can achieve a (1/2^{q-2})-approximation for LLP parity learning. These findings show qualitative differences between the LLP and PAC settings: problems tractable in PAC (e.g., ORs, parities) become inapproximable in LLP. The results rely on reductions from Label Cover and Smooth Label Cover problems. Prior works are discussed, highlighting that LLP learnability has been far less explored compared to PAC learning. Applications include privacy and weak supervision scenarios.\",\"docName\":\"2403.19401\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:57:36.527\",\"fileName\":\"2403.19401.pdf\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987776370834542592,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile