tModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"da0c0f7162b267cca0de596df4c40c56\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3nql1cs15r8aviz39pth2bpsr4zvy1.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\db8ff9222bdd0e56ba076310ced4c747.pdf\",\"priority\":2,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":402,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.639\",\"description\":\"Psychological identity encompasses self-image, self-esteem, and individuality, representing how individuals perceive themselves in the present, past, and future. According to Weinreich, identity is the totality of self-construal, reflecting continuity across time and aspirations. Ethnic identity, for example, is defined as the part of self-construal connecting ancestry and future goals in terms of ethnicity. The concept of identity is central to fields like psychology, sociology, and anthropology, requiring frameworks that address both unique personal attributes and shifting group affiliations. Weinreich’s Identity Structure Analysis (ISA) provides a structural approach to mapping identity, focusing on stable relationships between the self and others within socio-cultural contexts. ISA utilizes constructs from individual experience, group dynamics, and cultural norms, offering a methodology for analyzing how individuals use these elements to evaluate themselves, others, and institutions over time and in varying circumstances.\",\"docName\":\"3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:57:38.411\",\"fileName\":\"3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987776371153309696,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Informational\",\"Research Paper\",\"Psychology\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"a80f0ad4af9fd250a462680c85e6fbf0\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3u84xhcdicdb6vqtlfud7syhj444z2.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\4bb6e885c6b375f7991ed237ae550f5a.pdf\",\"priority\":3,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":414,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.666\",\"description\":\"Archie receives orders to join the earl and a large group of soldiers in pursuit of Bruce after a traitor reveals Bruce’s recent whereabouts. Despite his reluctance, Archie prepares to lead the party using Hector, the hound, whose tracking skills are crucial for the search. Archie contemplates killing the hound and fleeing if they get close to Bruce, though escape seems unlikely given the number of mounted men. Guided by a local peasant, the group reaches a cottage five miles away where Hector becomes highly excited, thoroughly investigating the area before decisively taking up Bruce’s trail. Archie struggles to control the eager hound, who pulls hard on the chain. He cautions Pembroke, the earl, to keep the knights and soldiers at a distance, noting that their noise further agitates Hector and makes the dog harder to manage. The narrative details the tension and urgency of the pursuit, highlighting Archie’s internal conflict and the hound’s pivotal role.\",\"docName\":\"3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:57:41.133\",\"fileName\":\"3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1.txt\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987776371266555904,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"History\",\"Report\",\"Text Document\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"bb28043dd606535bdcaf1ee30346b0f9\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\e6e2343232ca421ef2da852a5f84548c.pdf\",\"priority\":4,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":326,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.695\",\"description\":\"This document outlines the design method for evaluating AI face algorithms, focusing on test set collection and variable matrices. Key variables include head movements (pitch, yaw, roll), facial motions (mouth, eyebrow, eyelid), and lighting conditions (weak, medium, strong, uneven). Motion transfers involve horizontal and angular head movements, while expression transfers and optical flow are assessed under varied light environments. The evaluation uses only laptop cameras, with tools from Ruifeng for video input/output collection. Collection involves preparing 70 videos, at least 5 seconds each, covering head and facial movement variables and lighting conditions, with 10 videos per variable. Each variable should ideally have data from multiple participants, covering at least 7 individuals. Driving videos and output videos are compared to assess algorithm performance, using selected source images and ground truth from videos.\",\"docName\":\"AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:57:50.923\",\"fileName\":\"AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design.pptx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987776371388190720,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Technology\",\"Data Analysis\",\"Specifications\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:46:00\",\"md5\":\"b0f396e7936e0f6192cd880927e8e74b\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design.pptx\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\5820f6ff633a3666eab35d871b69006b.pdf\",\"priority\":5,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":432,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.726\",\"description\":\"\",\"docName\":\"A_brief_introduction_of_LP_liutao21_20220615\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:58:02.464\",\"fileName\":\"A_brief_introduction_of_LP_liutao21_20220615.pptx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987776371518214144,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:46:00\",\"md5\":\"0dbb2532524843af54fb3388b7bf3381\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\A_brief_introduction_of_LP_liutao21_20220615.pptx\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\d590d120fc4bcc9bc01d308c3ad03c90.pdf\",\"priority\":6,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":1297,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.800\",\"description\":\"\",\"docName\":\"\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:58:02.480\",\"fileName\":\"Belfast-English.docx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987776371828592640,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:45:00\",\"md5\":\"3193e699ffa92db332ae31a9fb1d3cb4\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\Belfast-English.docx\",\"priority\":7,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"RUNNING\",\"tokenNum\":0,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.823\",\"description\":\"\",\"docName\":\"\",\"e