XinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\3z3zlgnnsiuha76yy56h6uu71bh3q1.txt\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\e6e2343232ca421ef2da852a5f84548c.pdf\",\"priority\":4,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":326,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.695\",\"description\":\"This document outlines the design method for evaluating AI face algorithms, focusing on test set collection and variable matrices. Key variables include head movements (pitch, yaw, roll), facial motions (mouth, eyebrow, eyelid), and lighting conditions (weak, medium, strong, uneven). Motion transfers involve horizontal and angular head movements, while expression transfers and optical flow are assessed under varied light environments. The evaluation uses only laptop cameras, with tools from Ruifeng for video input/output collection. Collection involves preparing 70 videos, at least 5 seconds each, covering head and facial movement variables and lighting conditions, with 10 videos per variable. Each variable should ideally have data from multiple participants, covering at least 7 individuals. Driving videos and output videos are compared to assess algorithm performance, using selected source images and ground truth from videos.\",\"docName\":\"AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:57:50.923\",\"fileName\":\"AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design.pptx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987776371388190720,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Technology\",\"Data Analysis\",\"Specifications\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:46:00\",\"md5\":\"b0f396e7936e0f6192cd880927e8e74b\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\\\\AI_Face_Test_Set_Collection_Method_Design.pptx\",\"previewPath\":\"c:\\\\users\\\\xiaoxinpro 16 iah\\\\documents\\\\qtcore\\\\pdffile\\\\5820f6ff633a3666eab35d871b69006b.pdf\",\"priority\":5,\"score\":0.0,\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"tokenNum\":432,\"uriPath\":\"\"},{\"createTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:56:32.726\",\"description\":\"This document provides an introduction to Linear Programming (LP), detailing its definition, historical development, solution methods, and strategies for testing LP solvers. LP focuses on optimizing a linear objective function subject to linear constraints, with solutions within a convex polyhedral feasible set. Mixed-Integer Programming (MIP) extends LP by allowing some variables to be integers, solved using techniques like Branch-and-Bound. The history covers key contributors: G.B. Dantzig (concept, Simplex Method), L.V. Kantorovich and T.C. Koopmans (Nobel Prize), L.G. Khachian (Ellipsoid Method), and N. Karmarkar (Interior-Point Method). Solution methods include enumeration, the Simplex Method (effective in practice but exponential in worst cases), and the Interior-Point Method (polynomial-time efficiency for large problems). LP solvers are tested using both benchmark and self-built test sets, including classic optimization models with varying size and structure. Testing approaches include handling unique, infinite, infeasible, and unbounded problems, using different coefficient scales and constraints to evaluate solver robustness and correctness.\",\"docName\":\"A_brief_introduction_of_LP_liutao21_20220615\",\"editTime\":\"2025-11-10T14:58:06.320\",\"fileName\":\"A_brief_introduction_of_LP_liutao21_20220615.pptx\",\"folder\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\XiaoXinPro 16 IAH\\\\Desktop\\\\LRIA_llm_eval\\\\eval\\\\Quantum_e2e_v20251106\\\\Bvt1110\\\\bvtfiles\",\"id\":1987776371518214144,\"isDeleted\":0,\"isTmpFile\":false,\"labelNameList\":[\"Technology\",\"Specifications\",\"Training\"],\"lastModifyTime\":\"2025-09-17T09:46:00\",\"md5\":\"0dbb25325